exercises.solution:
```python
def neg(opens, u):
    return frozenset().union(*[w for w in opens if not (w & u)])

def is_boolean(opens, space):
    return all(u | neg(opens, u) == space for u in opens)
```

The union of open sets is open, so `neg(opens, u)` is the *largest* open set disjoint from `u`, which is the pseudo-complement $\neg u = u \Rightarrow \varnothing$. In the Sierpiński space `S`, `u = {1}` has $\neg u = \varnothing$, so excluded middle fails. In the discrete topology `D` every open set is also closed, and the algebra is Boolean.
